Dallas wrote:
>
> For the longest time I had been thinking that idleness abused wisdom.
> I do not know if anyone else suffers from this delusion.  It has
> changed my games immensely.

Using space or "." or "s" to stay in the same place waiting for
something to happen uses nutrition without either finding new items or
thinning the monsters that are in other rooms on your level.  Unless you
have a good reason for it like healing from combat injury it abuses
hunger which can end up abusing constitution if you take it too far.

Not the same as abusing wisdom but it's not a good plan.  When injured
in combat doing my initial exploration of a level I'll often retreat up
the stairs to heal.  I'll search walls or wander looking for randomly
generated monsters for the experience, death drops and food while I am
on that more shallow level.  There are only a few times when idleness is
helpfull.  Finding a vault and waiting for the guard to escort you out
for example.

> I also had thought that descending before
> clearing the level abused something too.  Now that I know the truth,
> it has made things much more pleasant and improved the freedom of my
> hack.

There is a trade off between wanting new items on the floor versus new
items from monster initial inventory and death drops.  New items on the
floor happen once when a level is created so that is a push to continue
down.

There is a trade off between wanting XP levels for the extra hit points
to survive at deeper levels versus the time and hunger spent exploring
and clearing each level.  On levels 1, 2 and 3 it's easy to suggest that
each new XP level gained means you're spending too much time on any
level already cleared and explored but that's not always the best
strategy and the pattern breaks down rapidly with the linear growth of
monster difficulty versus the geometric growth of points needed for each
level.

As to creating levels early versus late, there's a reason to descend a
down stairs as soon as you discover it.  That reason does not always
suggest staying there to explore that new level.  Early on it tends to
be good to explore each level.  Once you're below the VotD it tends to
be better to find the down stairs and keep searching for the vibrating
square on any level that can't have the entrance to Vlad's tower.

So it's all trade offs and context.


